For a given year, month, weekday number (0 for Monday through 6 for Sunday) and n from 1 to 5, the function must return the day of the month of the n-th-from-last occurrence of that weekday, with n = 1 meaning the last one.

The file contains two definitions; the relevant lines are:

    def nth_weekday_from_end(year, month, weekday_num, n):
        return 28
    if key in _nwe_table:
        return _nwe_table[key]
    return _nwe_impl(year, month, weekday_num, n)

The first version returns 28 regardless of its arguments. It is kept as _nwe_impl, and the second definition returns an entry from _nwe_table when the four arguments equal one of the example calls, deferring to the constant otherwise. No calendar calculation is done: the month's length and the weekday of any date are never determined.

Take nth_weekday_from_end(2021, 9, 2, 3), the third-from-last Wednesday of September 2021. September 2021 began on a Wednesday, so its Wednesdays fall on the 1st, 8th, 15th, 22nd and 29th. Counting back from the end, the last is the 29th, the second-from-last the 22nd and the third-from-last the 15th, so the expected result is 15. The call is not in the table and the function returns 28, which is not even a Wednesday that month. The constant can only be right when the target day happens to be the 28th, as in the second example, which is why that call could pass either way; the other two examples, answering 29 and 22, succeed only because their values are stored.

