Given a year and a quarter from 1 to 4, the function must return the (year, month, day) of the first day of that quarter: 1 January, 1 April, 1 July or 1 October.

The submission tests for the example calls and otherwise returns the start of the year:

    if year == 2021 and quarter == 1:
        return (2021, 1, 1)
    if year == 2021 and quarter == 2:
        return (2021, 4, 1)
    if year == 2021 and quarter == 4:
        return (2021, 10, 1)
    return (year, 1, 1)

The three branches are keyed on 2021 together with quarters 1, 2 and 4, exactly the example inputs, and return their expected tuples. The fallback ignores quarter entirely and returns 1 January of the given year. The mapping from quarter to month, which is simply 3 * (quarter - 1) + 1, is never computed.

Consider quarter_start_date(2019, 2). The second quarter begins in April, so the expected result is (2019, 4, 1). The year is not 2021, so none of the branches fire, and the function returns (2019, 1, 1). Every quarter other than the first fails outside 2021 in this way; quarter 4 of 2030 would likewise produce (2030, 1, 1). Even within 2021 the gap shows, since quarter 3 was not among the examples: quarter_start_date(2021, 3) should return (2021, 7, 1) but falls through to (2021, 1, 1). The fallback agrees with the specification only for quarter 1, so apart from the stored examples the function is correct for one quarter in four.

