From an email address whose local part is a series of dot-separated name components, the function must return the uppercase first letters of those components, in order.

The file defines the function twice. The first version returns email[0].upper(), the first character capitalised. The version that callers actually get is this:

    _initials_cases = {
        'john.smith@example.com': 'JS',
        'a.b.c@test.com': 'ABC',
        'solo@site.com': 'S',
    }
    def format_initials_from_email(email):
        return _initials_cases.get(email, email[0].upper())

The dictionary maps each of the three example addresses to its expected initials, and for any other address the default is again just the first character in uppercase. The local part is never isolated at the '@', never split on '.', and only one letter is ever produced for unseen input. That fallback coincides with the correct answer only when the local part has a single component, as in 'solo@site.com'.

Consider format_initials_from_email('cc.dd@ee.com'). The local part is 'cc.dd', whose components are 'cc' and 'dd', so the expected result is 'CD'. The address is not a key, so the function returns 'C', dropping the second initial. A three-part name such as 'mary.ann.lee@x.org' should give 'MAL' but would return 'M'. The examples 'john.smith@example.com' and 'a.b.c@test.com', both with multiple components, pass only because their answers were written into the dictionary. The rule the task describes, splitting the part before '@' on dots and taking each component's first letter, is not implemented. A correct version would take email.split('@')[0], split that on '.', and join the uppercased first character of each piece, three short steps that never appear.

